{"id":"neet-2025-q003","pn":3,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Velocity selector, crossed E and B","q":"An electron (mass 9 × 10⁻³¹ kg and charge 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) moving with speed c/100 (c = speed of light) is injected into a magnetic field B⃗ of magnitude 9 × 10⁻⁴ T perpendicular to its direction of motion. We wish to apply an uniform electric field E⃗ together with the magnetic field so that the electron does not deflect from its path. Then (speed of light c = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹)","o":["E⃗ is perpendicular to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹","E⃗ is perpendicular to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10² V m⁻¹","E⃗ is parallel to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10² V m⁻¹","E⃗ is parallel to B⃗ and its magnitude is 27 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"For no deflection of the electron, F_B = F_E.\n−e(v⃗ × B⃗) = −eE⃗\n⇒ E⃗ = v⃗ × B⃗ ⇒ E⃗ ⊥ B⃗\nE = vB = (c/100) × 9 × 10⁻⁴\n= (3 × 10⁸/100) × 9 × 10⁻⁴\n= 27 × 10² V m⁻¹","sfig":{"w":190,"h":210,"alt":"An electron moving right with velocity v; the force F_E on it points up and F_B points down; B points into the page (two crossed circles) and E points down.","d":[["c",25.6,83.2,16,""],["t",25.6,87.2,"e⁻","m"],["l",41.6,83.2,104,83.2,"da"],["t",110.4,87.2,"v",""],["l",25.6,67.2,25.6,20,"a"],["t",21.6,13.6,"F",""],["t",28.8,16.8,"E","s"],["l",25.6,99.2,25.6,145.6,"a"],["t",21.6,161.6,"F",""],["t",28.8,164.8,"B","s"],["c",78.4,28,8,""],["l",73.6,23.2,83.2,32.8,""],["l",83.2,23.2,73.6,32.8,""],["c",101.6,28,8,""],["l",96.8,23.2,106.4,32.8,""],["l",106.4,23.2,96.8,32.8,""],["t",125.6,33.6,"B",""],["t",125.6,24,"→","s"],["l",146.4,86.4,146.4,132,"a"],["l",169.6,86.4,169.6,132,"a"],["t",152.8,153.6,"E",""],["t",152.8,144,"→","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q012","pn":12,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Magnetic moment of a current loop","q":"A 2 amp current is flowing through two different small circular copper coils having radii ratio 1 : 2. The ratio of their respective magnetic moments will be","o":["1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1","4 : 1"],"c":0,"s":"Magnetic moment of a current carrying circular loop = IA\nM = IA\nM ∝ A [I is the same]\nM₁/M₂ = A₁/A₂ = πr₁²/πr₂² = (1/2)² = 1/4"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q018","pn":18,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","st":"Magnetic moment of orbiting electron","q":"A model for quantized motion of an electron in a uniform magnetic field B states that the flux passing through the orbit of the electron is n(h/e) where n is an integer, h is Planck’s constant and e is the magnitude of electron’s charge. According to the model, the magnetic moment of an electron in its lowest energy state will be (m is the mass of the electron)","o":["he/(πm)","he/(2πm)","heB/(πm)","heB/(2πm)"],"c":1,"s":"Magnetic force provides the centripetal force: evB = mv²/r\nv = eBr/m\nFlux φ = BA, so nh/e = Bπr²\nBr² = nh/(eπ)\nμ = IA = (e/T)πr² = (e × v/(2πr))πr²\nμ = evr/2 = (1/2) e × (eBr/m) × r\nμ = (1/2)e² (Br²/m) = (1/2)e² (nh/(eπm))\nμ = neh/(2πm)\nFor n = 1 (lowest state): μ = eh/(2πm)"}
